<article class="env-exercise" id="0EGP">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0EGP">Exercise <span data-tag="0EGP">111.47.1</span>.</a> Let $k$ be an algebraically closed field. Let $d \geq 1$ and $n \geq 1$. Let us say the moduli of hypersurfaces of degree $d$ in $P^ n$ is given by </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
an object is a hypersurface $X \subset \mathbf{P}^ n_ k$ of degree $d$, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
an isomorphism between two objects $X \subset \mathbf{P}^ n_ k$ and $Y \subset \mathbf{P}^ n_ k$ is an element $g \in \text{PGL}_ n(k)$ such that $g(X) = Y$, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
a family of hypersurfaces over a variety $V$ is a closed subscheme $X \subset \mathbf{P}^ n_ V$ such that for all $v \in V$ the scheme theoretic fibre $X_ v$ of $X \to V$ is a hypersurfaces in $\mathbf{P}^ n_ v$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> Compute (if you can – these get progressively harder) </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
the number of moduli when $n = 1$ and $d$ arbitrary, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
the number of moduli when $n = 2$ and $d = 1$, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
the number of moduli when $n = 2$ and $d = 2$, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
the number of moduli when $n \geq 1$ and $d = 2$, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
the number of moduli when $n = 2$ and $d = 3$, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
the number of moduli when $n = 3$ and $d = 3$, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
the number of moduli when $n = 2$ and $d = 4$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article>